{title: Watchman, Tell Us of the Night}
{artist: John Bowring / Lowell Mason}
{genre: Christmas}
{style: Traditional}
{flow: Opening}
{key: D}
{tempo: 65}
{time: 3/2}
{c: Chords checked against the four-part harmony of the Cyber Hymnal score (The Book of Hymns (Nashville, Tennessee: United Methodist Publishing House, 1966), # 358)}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[D]Watchman, tell us of the night,
[D]What its signs of prom[F#m]ise are.__[A]__
[D]Traveler, o’er yon mountain’s height,
[D]See [G]that glory [D]beaming star.
[D]Watchman, does its beauteous ray
[D]Aught of joy__[Bm]__ or [A]hope foretell?
[D]Traveler, yes— it brings the day,
[D]Prom[G]ised day of [D]Israel.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[D]Watchman, tell us of the night;
[D]Higher yet that star [F#m]ascends.__[A]__
[D]Traveler, blessedness and light,
[D]Peace [G]and truth its [D]course portends.
[D]Watchman, will its beams alone
[D]Gild the spot__[Bm]__ that [A]gave them birth?
[D]Traveler, ages are its own;
[D]See, [G]it bursts o’er [D]all the earth.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[D]Watchman, tell us of the night,
[D]For the morning seems [F#m]to dawn.__[A]__
[D]Traveler, darkness takes its flight,
[D]Doubt [G]and terror [D]are withdrawn.
[D]Watchman, let thy wanderings cease;
[D]Hie thee to__[Bm]__ thy [A]quiet home.
[D]Traveler, lo! the Prince of Peace,
[D]Lo! [G]the Son of [D]God is come!
{end_of_verse}
